[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Introduction"]
[Black "Preface"]
[Result "*"]

{ @@HeaderStart@@Preface@@HeaderEnd@@  After writing my exercise book “The Chess Manual of Avoidable Mistakes – Volume 2”, I wanted to continue in the same spirit by launching another series of books: “Chess Calculation Training”. This first volume will focus on middlegames.  I have always found that existing puzzle books, however good they are, only provide a rather mechanical form of mental training. In this new collection, you will find exercises to help you improve in specific areas of thinking, such as you may encounter in your own games. To help this, all the problems are taken from real games, with most of them having taken place in the last year.   The book begins with a warm-up. After that, you will find several types of exercises. Some of these fall into the same categories as those in my previous book, as they were readers’ favourites. Other categories are completely new, for instance: “Find the killer positional move!” or “Find the stunning winning move!”.  The problems in the book are accessible to players with a level from modest to confident. The most difficult ones are marked with an asterisk, while the most challenging chapter comes with a help page, that you may check when necessary.  I hope you enjoy solving these 496 puzzles, and look forward to seeing you again in Volume 2: Endgames.  Romain Edouard  March 1st 2017 }
1. -- *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Introduction"]
[Black "Chessable Foreword"]
[Result "*"]

{ As is typical of our conversions to Chessable, we have tried to remain as faithful to the original print book as possible. All the content is included in an interactive, trainable format.  For the first 7 chapters, this book follows the usual format of normal tactics/calculation books, where you are tasked with finding the best continuation in that position. The last 4 chapters are especially exciting however, since you are posed with questions.  For example, in:  - 'Find the Missed Move!' you need to find what has been missed in a sequence of moves.  - 'Evaluate the Opportunity' you have to decide whether the proposed move is good or not.  - 'Make the Right Choice!' you are allowed to choose which of the two suggested moves is best.  - 'Special Section' you are asked a variety of questions pertaining to each position.  Since most of these questions are not answerable with a single playable move, we opted for a slightly different approach here. You are first presented with the question, and once you have come up with a solution, simply click on the next page to check if you were right.  If you notice any errors, inaccuracies or things we could improve, please let us know.   Enjoy the book! }
1. -- *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Warm Up"]
[Black "Introduction: Warm Up"]
[Result "*"]

{ @@HeaderStart@@Introduction: Warm Up@@HeaderEnd@@  As the title indicates, this part of the book is a warm up. For each position given, you must find the winning move. If there seem to be more than one, you should go for the quickest and/or the clearest.  This chapter contains exercises of all types, which could have been used in several of the other chapters. They have been collected in this chapter because of their lower difficulty and will prepare you nicely for the rest of the book!   This chapter is quite long: I recommend attempting one or two pages  (4 to 8 exercises)  as a daily routine, or as a warm-up before looking at another chapter. }
1. -- *

